'''absque hoc''' (abz′kwē hok). [L., without this (or that): ''absque'', without, < ''abs'', off, from, with generalizing suffix ''-que''; ''hoc'', abl. of ''hic'', this, that.] Without this or that: specifically used, in ''law'', in traversing what has been alleged and is repeated.

'''absque tali causa''' (abz′kwē tā′lī kâ′zä̤). [L.: ''absque'', without; ''tali'', abl. of ''talis'', such; ''causa'', abl. of ''causa'', cause.] Without such cause: a phrase used in law.

'''abs. re.''' In ''law'', an abbreviation of Latin ''absente reo'' (which see), the defendant being absent.

'''abstain''' (ab-stān′), ''v.'' [< ME. ''abstainen'', ''absteinen'', ''abstenen'', < OF. ''abstener'', ''abstenir'', ''astenir'', F. ''abstenir'', refl., < L. ''abstinēre'', abstain, < ''abs'', off, + ''tenēre'', hold: see ''tenable''. Cf. ''contain'', ''attain'', ''detain'', ''pertain'', ''retain'', ''sustain''.] '''I.''' ''intrans.'' To forbear or refrain voluntarily, especially from what gratifies the passions or appetites: used with ''from'': as, to ''abstain from'' the use of ardent spirits; to ''abstain from'' luxuries.
{{smaller block|''Abstain from'' meats offered to idols.{{right|Acts xv. 29.}}
To walk well, it is not enough that a man ''abstains from'' dancing.{{right|''De Quincey'', Herodotus.}}}}
'''II.'''† ''trans.'' To hinder; obstruct; debar;

